EXT. DRY-DOCK SHIPYARD - ROWING TANK - DAWN

A vast concrete basin, drained and refilled with grey-green water, used by the old shipyard for hull testing and now, secretly, as a rowing course. Rusted cranes loom like skeletal herons. Mist lies on the surface.

A racing shell sits at the dock, a single scull, polished cedar. Beside it stands NAOMI BERENSON (30s, Olympic hopeful, lean, hair cropped) in a rain jacket, hands on hips, studying the water. Her breath fogs. Her calf muscles twitch. She is alone, and she has been alone for a long time.

She crouches, tests the water with two fingers, wipes them on her thigh. She sets the scull in the water and lowers herself into it with one fluid movement. The oars dip.

She begins to row.

The scull glides across the basin. Her stroke is long and exact. The mist parts, closes behind her. A gull cries. Somewhere beyond the cranes a siren is heard and fades.

She reaches the far end, turns the shell with two pulls, and comes back. Faster now. The oars bite deeper. Her shoulders flare, her breath hitches, and the water boils behind the blades.

At the dock, a figure has appeared. A woman in a heavy wool coat, thermos in hand, standing absolutely still. This is COACH ILSE VORHEES (60s, grey crop, stern as a gravestone). She watches the scull approach.

Naomi sees her. Her stroke falters. The right oar catches the water at a bad angle and the boat veers. She corrects it. Her breath is ragged.

COACH VORHEES
(calling)
Don't stop.

NAOMI
(between breaths)
I'm not stopping.

COACH VORHEES
Then why are you slowing?

NAOMI
I'm not.

COACH VORHEES
Look at the clock.

Naomi glances at the waterproof watch strapped to the bow. Her face falls. Six minutes fifty. Not good enough.

NAOMI
I'm having an off day.

COACH VORHEES
You've been having an off day for a year.

The scull glides the last twenty metres. Naomi ships the oars, lets the boat drift to the dock, and sits there gasping. Vorhees crouches, extends a hand. Naomi takes it and is hauled out onto the concrete. She sits against a bollard, chest heaving.

NAOMI
Why are you here?

COACH VORHEES
To tell you that Beijing is eleven weeks away.

NAOMI
I know when Beijing is.

COACH VORHEES
Do you? Because you row like someone who has forgotten.

Naomi stares at the water. The scull rocks gently at its mooring. Vorhees unscrews the thermos and pours tea into the cap. She hands it over.

COACH VORHEES (CONT'D)
Drink.

Naomi drinks. The steam rises between them.

NAOMI
I think I'm done.

COACH VORHEES
You think.

NAOMI
I know.

COACH VORHEES
Then say it properly.

Naomi looks at her. Looks away. The cranes, the mist, the grey water.

NAOMI
I can't do it anymore.

Silence. The thermos cap trembles in her hands. Vorhees does not speak for a long time. Then she sits down on the concrete beside her, heavily, like a woman putting down a load she has carried too long.

COACH VORHEES
Do you know why I came this morning?

NAOMI
To tell me I'm slow.

COACH VORHEES
To tell you that I stopped timing you in March.

Naomi turns, slowly.

COACH VORHEES (CONT'D)
Every split since March was a lie. I wrote down numbers I knew you'd hit. You've been faster than you think for months.

NAOMI
That's not funny.

COACH VORHEES
I'm not laughing.

NAOMI
Why?

COACH VORHEES
Because you needed someone to lie to you.

The sun breaks over the cranes. Light floods the basin. The mist burns off in a single rolling wave, and the water flashes gold. Naomi starts to laugh. It turns into something between laughing and weeping. Vorhees puts an arm around her. Neither of them looks at the other.

NAOMI
You're awful.

COACH VORHEES
I'm your coach.

Naomi wipes her face with her sleeve. She looks at the scull, already steaming in the sun.

NAOMI
Again?

COACH VORHEES
Again.
